.TH GRUBFORGE 1 "October 2026" "grubForge v2.2.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
grubforge \- the GRUB boot menu, without editing files by hand
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grubforge
.br
.B grubforge \-\-version
.br
.B grubforge \-\-help
.br
.B sudo grubforge
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B grubForge
is a keyboard-driven terminal application for the GRUB bootloader.
Every setting has a plain name and an explanation, known values are picked
from lists, and every change is reviewed (old \(-> new) and backed up before
it is written.
.PP
Saving and rebuilding are separate.
Saving writes
.I /etc/default/grub
(and, for a boot order,
.IR /etc/grub.d/40_custom );
rebuilding runs
.BR grub-mkconfig (8)
(or
.B grub2-mkconfig
where the distribution uses it) so the boot menu picks the change up.
Until it is rebuilt, grubForge shows
.I \(dqSaved \(bu not in the boot menu yet\(dq
and asks before quitting.
.PP
grubForge runs as an ordinary user.
When a change needs to modify
.I /etc
or
.IR /boot ,
it asks
.BR polkit (8),
and since 2.1 grubForge asks for the password in its own box, as polkit's
authentication agent for its own process only; polkit's helper checks it.
This works on a desktop and on a text console alike.
The only component running as root is
.IR /usr/lib/grubforge/grubforge-helper ,
which accepts a fixed list of named operations and cannot be given a
command to run.
.PP
The full manual is inside the application (press
.BR M ,
or
.B F1
on anything for its page) and in
.I /usr/lib/grubforge/grubforge/manual/
as Markdown.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.
.SH USAGE
.TP
.B grubforge
Open grubForge. Changes are authorised through polkit when saved.
.TP
.B sudo grubforge
Run with root privileges directly. Only needed over SSH, where grubForge's
polkit rule refuses permission (since 2.1 a text console asks in grubForge's own box).
.PP
When grubForge closes it prints a short record: what was saved, whether
the boot menu has it, the newest backup, and where the run was logged.
.
.SH SCREENS
.TP
.B 1 \- Overview
Your boot menu at a glance, what needs attention (with the button that
fixes it), safety (backups, password, where GRUB lives) and common tasks.
.TP
.B 2 \- Settings
Every GRUB setting as a form in five groups: Start-up, Look, Kernel options,
Other systems, Advanced. A changed setting is marked and shows its old
value. On Debian and Ubuntu, a setting decided in
.I /etc/default/grub.d/
is shown locked with the file that sets it.
.TP
.B 3 \- Boot Menu
The entries as shown at start-up. Move, rename, choose what starts first,
remove, add an entry (checked with
.BR grub-script-check ),
find other systems. Entries made by other tools are fixed in place.
.TP
.B 4 \- Themes
Installed themes with a preview of your entries in the theme's colours;
use a theme, or install a downloaded one.
.TP
.B 5 \- Backups
Backups with the reason each was made and what restoring it would change.
.TP
.B 6 \- Help
The manual, the list of keys, the license and About.
.
.SH KEYBINDINGS
.TP
.B Tab ", " Shift+Tab
Next / previous field or button.
.TP
.B Enter
Open a list, press a button, confirm.
.TP
.B Space
Flip a switch, tick a box.
.TP
.B Esc
Close a window.
.TP
.B 1 \(en 6 ", " Ctrl+letter
Go to a menu entry, in the order of the menu bar (6 is Help); Ctrl + the
entry's underlined letter does the same.
.TP
.B F10 ", " S
Save, with a review first.
.TP
.B F9 ", " Ctrl+R
Rebuild the boot menu.
.TP
.B R
Read the files again.
.TP
.B F1
Help on what is selected.
.TP
.B M
The manual.
.TP
.B ?
All keys.
.TP
.B Q ", " Ctrl+Q
Quit; asks first if something is unsaved, or saved but not rebuilt.
.TP
.B Shift+Up ", " Shift+Down ", " F2 ", " + ", " F
Boot Menu: move, rename, add an entry, find other systems.
.TP
.B I
Themes: install a theme.
.TP
.B N ", " R ", " D
Backups: back up now, restore, delete.
.PP
A button shows its key in brackets after its name, for example
.B Save Changes (s)
or
.BR "Rebuild Boot Menu (F9)" .
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/grub
GRUB's settings. Read and written by grubForge.
.TP
.I /etc/default/grub.d/*.cfg
Debian and Ubuntu: read by grub-mkconfig after
.IR /etc/default/grub ;
settings decided there are shown, locked.
.TP
.IR /boot/grub/grub.cfg ", " /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
The boot menu, rebuilt by grub-mkconfig or grub2-mkconfig; never edited
directly.
.TP
.IR /boot/grub/themes/ ", " /boot/grub2/themes/
Installed themes.
.TP
.I /etc/grub.d/40_custom
Your boot order, when you save one.
.TP
.I /var/lib/grubforge/backups/
Backups of
.I /etc/default/grub
(the last 10), each with a copy of
.I 40_custom
beside it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/grubforge/logs/
One line per run, by day.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/grubforge/grubforge-helper
The privileged helper, run through
.BR pkexec (1).
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.kognogos.grubforge.policy
The polkit action
.RB ( org.kognogos.grubforge.manage ).
.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux with GRUB; Python 3.10 or newer; python-textual, python-rich and
python-forgekit 0.10.0 or newer; polkit; python-gobject (for the password
box).
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grub-mkconfig (8),
.BR grub-install (8),
.BR os-prober (8),
.BR pkexec (1),
.BR polkit (8)
.
